{"reply":"There’s a striking connection here, with an important difference. In Chapter 8, “The Queen’s Croquet-Ground,” the gardeners are painting the roses because they planted the wrong tree and fear the Queen’s punishment: “if the Queen was to find it out, we should all have our heads cut off.” Their correction is also an attempt to conceal the error. But the Queen does find out—“I see!”—and orders their execution. So the scene isn’t about successful secrecy; it shows how threatening authority can make concealment feel like the safest available response, even when it may not work.

Your billing note has a quieter version of that structure: you found the mistake, fixed it, and said nothing. That does not establish that anyone threatened you, and the fictional punishment is obviously extreme. Still, the comparison may help identify what your reaction is responding to: perhaps not only the error, but the perceived risk attached to disclosing it.

Research on error reporting—this particular study concerns nurses and hospital organisations—finds that fear, perceived interpersonal risk, psychological safety, safety climate, and leaders’ openness all affect people’s willingness to report mistakes ([Munn et al., 2023](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/)). It supports a way of examining your response, not a diagnosis of your motive: when you stayed quiet, what consequence did speaking seem to carry, and what told you that disclosure was—or wasn’t—safe?","evidence_uses":[{"supported_claims":["In Chapter 8, “The Queen’s Croquet-Ground,” the gardeners are painting the roses because they planted the wrong tree and fear the Queen’s punishment: “if the Queen was to find it out, we should all have our heads cut off.” Their correction is also an attempt to conceal the error."],"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch08-ln1931-1970","source_location":"Chapter 8 — The Queen’s Croquet-Ground, source lines 1931-1970","exact_quote":"“if the Queen was to find it out, we should all have our heads cut off.”"},{"supported_claims":["But the Queen does find out—“I see!”—and orders their execution."],"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch08-ln2018-2074","source_location":"Chapter 8 — The Queen’s Croquet-Ground, source lines 2018-2074","exact_quote":"“I see!”"},{"supported_claims":["Your billing note has a quieter version of that structure: you found the mistake, fixed it, and said nothing."],"source_kind":"memory","evidence_id":"mem_997d4f30d53f4accd94f99d8b14abfc4ca5208d873d0ab98bd7cb1dfeb83fdf8","exact_quote":null},{"supported_claims":["Research on error reporting—this particular study concerns nurses and hospital organisations—finds that fear, perceived interpersonal risk, psychological safety, safety climate, and leaders’ openness all affect people’s willingness to report mistakes."],"source_kind":"web","evidence_id":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/","ex